hang upon the pegs, or lean against them.  There is a bar across the rack,

with a lock at one end, and a hinge at the other.

"
E
"swords"
"Several swords are kept upon this rack.  Each one is well maintained, and

ready for use.  The rack's locking bar is down and firmly holding the weapons

in place.

"
E
"polearms"
"Standing in ranks are several varieties of polearm.  There are spears, pikes,

halberds, voulges, pole-axes, glaives, and tridents.  All of which are bound

under the locking bar.

"
E
"bows"
"Several bows, mostly shortbows, fill this short rack.  There is a small wooden

box next to the rack, it must contain the bowstrings, as the bows are not

strung.

"
E
"ladder"
"The ladder consists of a stout iron frame, bolted to the wall, and wooden

rungs.  The frame is about three feet wide, ten feet tall, and three inches

thick.  Each of the rungs is a board, slid in through the side of the ladder

frame, and strapped firmly in place with hemp rope.

"
